WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.365
Dreams of Memphis….

00:00:03.365 --> 00:00:06.894
(swimming in the oil of Olivetti)

00:00:07.894 --> 00:00:13.174
The suburbs were molded, plastic ambition; nylon bedrooms in boxes for the

00:00:13.174 --> 00:00:18.815
farmless and popcultured. They crawled out of the Great Depression, walked away

00:00:18.815 --> 00:00:23.877
from World War II, and escaped on Eisenhower expressways. They were the

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:29.591
gallery and grave for mid century design — the great stuff you’re thinking about

00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:34.726
and the crap that we plow into landfills. The suburbs danced polychromed

00:00:34.726 --> 00:00:40.439
wallflowers in the age of disco — but by 1986 they were the whitest breakdancers

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:45.936
on Earth — sticking as they tried to slide — while their urban contemporaries

00:00:45.936 --> 00:00:51.722
flowed on waxed floors flooded with warm margarine. They wore mirrored sunglasses

00:00:51.722 --> 00:00:57.001
and puffy, silver coats, rode day-glow skateboards, and talked on wireless

00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:02.136
telephones (powered by nickel cadmium batteries) while grilling animals.

00:01:02.136 --> 00:01:07.172
High school students wrote terrible essays, with pencils that featured large

00:01:07.172 --> 00:01:12.343
rubbers in the shape of Space Shuttles. They used Reagan vocabularies to argue

00:01:12.343 --> 00:01:17.580
that Sean Connery was the best Bond and William of Baskerville—even though they

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.550
never actually smelled that particular Rose. These papers were always first

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:27.989
drafts, masquerading as thirds; precisely three pages of fat spacing, with breaded

00:01:27.989 --> 00:01:33.293
summaries fried into anemic remainders, below simple sugar bodies. They tried to

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:38.195
think of words like semiotics and postmodern, but the only words that ever

00:01:38.195 --> 00:01:42.493
came were neon Pontiac and Firebird, floating on clouds of smoke.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:47.506
The Super Big Gulp had finally landed at the local 7-Eleven; it was on the news

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:52.520
the night before, and Chuck was in line behind a half dozen grown men and a few

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.276
twelve and thirteen year olds, curious about how it would work to hoist two

00:01:57.276 --> 00:02:02.289
pounds of Coca–Cola around with you all day. He wondered how long would it take

00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:07.238
to drink and whether the wax coating on the paper cup would hold up that long.

00:02:07.238 --> 00:02:12.316
Had they waxed the seams after the cup was fabricated? Would the massive glucose

00:02:12.316 --> 00:02:17.265
spikes and eventual crashes affect your ability to hold the cup? And how about

00:02:17.265 --> 00:02:22.279
these little kids—were they strong enough to lift it, and would they drop it as

00:02:22.279 --> 00:02:27.228
their sugar levels plunged? While the citric acid in the clear sodas shouldn’t

00:02:27.228 --> 00:02:31.598
be much of a problem, would the phosphoric acid in dark sodas rot our

00:02:31.598 --> 00:02:36.805
teeth out before we get to the bottom of the first Gulp? Chuck was calculating the

00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:41.561
proportional pH difference between various flavors when a man in a stocking

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:45.867
cap peeked around his right shoulder and asked, “Have you ever had a

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:46.767
colonoscopy?”.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:51.884
Christina was still thinking about the guy with the high waisted jeans and the

00:02:51.884 --> 00:02:57.001
white long sleeved shirt tucked into it. He had taken his white paper register

00:02:57.001 --> 00:03:01.653
receipt, with the red stripes that indicated the roll was nearly empty,

00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:06.903
crumpled it up to his fist and deposited it into her aunt’s hand without a word.

00:03:06.903 --> 00:03:12.020
“Why would someone be that rude?!” She hit a speed bump, hard, and looked down

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.805
to find her Christmas fuchsia WM-4 smashed against the curb, disconnected

00:03:16.805 --> 00:03:21.656
from its headphones. The dot matrix, textured, metal play button lay three

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:26.440
feet from the black, granite colored riser it used to project out of. She

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:31.624
should have been afraid the same would happen to the now unattached headphones,

00:03:31.624 --> 00:03:36.542
or her bicycle, or her face; but she fixated on the twenty-seven pieces her

00:03:36.542 --> 00:03:41.459
new Walkman was divided into by the collision. The tape door was cracked in

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:46.643
two, with the window about to subdivide by delamination. The pulleys and motors

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:51.760
and circuit boards spilled out like guts, stabbed by bicycle bayonets. The top

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.744
surface was intact, with the green bezels still outlining the two 3mm stereo

00:03:56.744 --> 00:04:01.861
headphone ports. The only damage there was that the METAL/NORM selector button

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:06.646
had popped off. The battery tray, designed to hold 4 AAs, had been turned

00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:11.630
inside out, with the paper sticker detailing the serial number holding on by

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:16.348
a corner. 108396 would go into a box to be repaired, but never would be.

00:04:16.348 --> 00:04:21.492
Her older brother, Kevin, wanted to stab his professor in the throat with his

00:04:21.492 --> 00:04:27.041
fork. Their parents told him he had to go to college, but he wasn’t up for a lot of

00:04:27.041 --> 00:04:32.185
reading; so an associates degree in culinary arts seemed like a great idea at

00:04:32.185 --> 00:04:36.922
the time. But he soon found the reading his friends were doing in their

00:04:36.922 --> 00:04:42.201
university courses seemed a lot easier than scrubbing deep fryers and having to

00:04:42.201 --> 00:04:47.480
memorize and modify batter recipes—and possibly more enjoyable. This Eatiquette

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:52.489
class (SCCA—302) made him crazy; food wasn’t the most punk rock field to go

00:04:52.489 --> 00:04:57.903
into in the first place; but doing it all in a particular way—with skirted tables

00:04:57.903 --> 00:05:02.708
and eight pieces of flatware, and having to read dense excerpts from The

00:05:02.708 --> 00:05:08.055
Civilizing Process—was making him nuts. They were forced to serve and eat entire

00:05:08.055 --> 00:05:13.334
meals this way, in uniform, exchanging anachronistic niceties in a post Back to

00:05:13.334 --> 00:05:18.681
the Future America. Everyone seemed to love it but him. “If that ASSHOLE motions

00:05:18.681 --> 00:05:24.230
for me to take my elbow off the table one more god-damned time, I am going to light

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.193
him on fire with his Zippo.”.

00:05:26.193 --> 00:05:31.773
Across town, their chubby thirteen year old cousin closed the door of his bedroom

00:05:31.773 --> 00:05:37.353
and pushed a small chair in front of it, but not so tight that it would seem that

00:05:37.353 --> 00:05:42.933
he’d trying to keep anyone out. He used an unmailed envelope that read Hillsboro,

00:05:42.933 --> 00:05:48.374
Oregon 97123 as a bookmark, marking a page toward the back of the July issue of

00:05:48.374 --> 00:05:53.884
Playboy. He turned the magazine open to find one of the sexiest ad sections he’d

00:05:53.884 --> 00:05:58.976
ever seen. It’s not that he wasn’t interested in Lynn Austin’s centerfold,

00:05:58.976 --> 00:06:04.486
or the cover girl (Carrie Leigh,) or the Salvador Dali painting of a Playmate—it

00:06:04.486 --> 00:06:10.067
was the spiritual redemption on offer in a particular advertisement. A photograph

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:15.507
showed a man taking off his shirt, arms above his head, the muscles on the left

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:20.529
side of his body in shadow, the right side etiolated by a floodlight. The

00:06:20.529 --> 00:06:25.551
background gradated in the opposite direction, with the right side of the

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:31.062
rectangle filled with solid black, fading to near white on the left. He imagined

00:06:31.062 --> 00:06:36.502
his belly shrinking to match the near X frame of the model’s, and he had become

00:06:36.502 --> 00:06:41.734
sure that the only way to achieve this dream was to perform the “twenty-four

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:47.035
traditional iron pumping exercises, each correct in form and balance” on this

00:06:47.035 --> 00:06:52.336
compact steel, wood, and rubber unit, that “fits in the corner of your home.”

00:06:52.336 --> 00:06:57.288
Tucked into the page was the free brochure and VHS tape he’d received by

00:06:57.288 --> 00:07:02.729
calling Soloflex® at 1-800-547-8802. But, while he’d already stolen the numbers

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:07.611
from his mother’s credit card, he hesitated before completing the order

00:07:07.611 --> 00:07:12.494
form—unsure as to whether or not to order any additional weight straps.

00:07:12.494 --> 00:07:18.350
“Am I the only one here who likes to read with my headphones on?!” The Suzzallo

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:23.831
library was a church of books—tall, college gothic, lit like a movie about

00:07:23.831 --> 00:07:29.386
the middle ages, with tables like pews and chairs made of rock wood. It was

00:07:29.386 --> 00:07:35.092
silent, but creaked like the dead. By noon, most days, it was packed with the

00:07:35.092 --> 00:07:41.023
loudest people in Seattle. Marcia Pazzi’s headphones went silent as the heads in

00:07:41.023 --> 00:07:47.104
her MW-F73 Walkman flipped to play side B of her Pretty in Pink soundtrack. As the

00:07:47.104 --> 00:07:52.585
tape started spinning again, decoding magnetic signals, sending electrical

00:07:52.585 --> 00:07:58.141
impulses to tiny full range speakers packed in foam pads, she continued her

00:07:58.141 --> 00:08:03.621
research for ARTH382. “…artists of all times, at their most inspired, have

00:08:03.621 --> 00:08:09.778
transported to a heavenly transfiguration precisely those ideas that we now know to

00:08:09.778 --> 00:08:15.333
be false: artists glorify mankind’s religious and philosophical errors, and

00:08:15.333 --> 00:08:20.889
they could not have done so without believing in their absolute truth.” She

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:26.595
was pretty sure one of the errors was the idea that one could only understand

00:08:26.595 --> 00:08:32.526
something in the absence of everything else—that one could better concentrate on

00:08:32.526 --> 00:08:38.081
reading if they were in silence. She scribbled furiously into a purple clad

00:08:38.081 --> 00:08:43.562
notebook—imagining the fights this passage would cause when she laid in on

00:08:43.562 --> 00:08:45.514
all the studio art majors.

00:08:45.514 --> 00:08:50.232
A father hunched over a pantograph machine, tracing a large, precise

00:08:50.232 --> 00:08:55.795
ligature with a stylus. With each curve, or dip, or stop, a motor connected to a

00:08:55.795 --> 00:09:01.287
reducing arm ground away a tool steel blank with a conical mill bit. He kept it

00:09:01.287 --> 00:09:06.639
well lubricated, with an oil that flowed like cranberry sauce. He was humming

00:09:06.639 --> 00:09:11.920
Smuggler’s Blues to himself, thinking about what Lawrence Fishburne’s prison

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.920
guard said to Philip Michael Thomas’s prisoner. “Back to the jungle,” is

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.342
exactly what his tenth grade English teacher had said when she recommended him

00:09:22.342 --> 00:09:27.764
for expulsion. It had been a decade since that typed declaration dried, but he

00:09:27.764 --> 00:09:32.834
could still taste iron in the ink, and the blood that flowed from it. The

00:09:32.834 --> 00:09:37.834
machine room buzzed to his busy, liquor-addled hum. Every few minutes he

00:09:37.834 --> 00:09:42.904
would let his foot off the rheostat controlling his instrument, check his

00:09:42.904 --> 00:09:48.114
well-lit progress under magnification, and then continue grinding until the

00:09:48.114 --> 00:09:53.255
stylized letters, PMD, read backwards in the end of the gray blank, with a

00:09:53.255 --> 00:09:58.607
precision that only 10X optics could verify. He had gone far too deep, and he

00:09:58.607 --> 00:10:01.212
could see it—but he couldn’t feel it.

00:10:01.212 --> 00:10:05.860
A band practiced in a newly erected mini-storage complex, the doors

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:10.861
half-closed to keep the guy in the cabinet-making unit from complaining.

00:10:10.861 --> 00:10:16.002
This wasn’t one of those bands that needed you to help them come up with a

00:10:16.002 --> 00:10:21.637
name; they weren’t a MeToo trio, looking for a formula or just playing out to get

00:10:21.637 --> 00:10:27.201
laid. They also hadn’t known each other since they were twelve, and none of them

00:10:27.201 --> 00:10:32.202
were siblings, and they didn’t have a plan. They had notebooks, they had

00:10:32.202 --> 00:10:37.625
parents who were struggling an average amount, and one of them was going to be

00:10:37.625 --> 00:10:43.330
starting grad school the following fall. On one of the notebooks was Kevin’s name,

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:49.035
schizophrenically ornamented with spirals and darts — overlapping and occasionally

00:10:49.035 --> 00:10:54.106
mirrored Ks and Ps, mixed with Vs, surrounded by frames of foliage. Venus

00:10:54.106 --> 00:10:59.740
screamed, “NANCY, leave me alone!” three times before the drummer decided to beat

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:04.741
out an unplanned solo. Her irises were Spirograph textured Crayola blue,

00:11:04.741 --> 00:11:09.812
surrounded by tan skin, chromed with purple shadows — fabulous and bored.

00:11:09.812 --> 00:11:15.376
There was no one to be dressed up for, but she wore a black fishnet shirt over a

00:11:15.376 --> 00:11:20.940
white tank top, rolled jean shorts over cow print leggings, a necklace made of a

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:26.152
blood pressure cuff with gauge, bulb and tubing, and a pair of metallic red

00:11:26.152 --> 00:11:31.504
KangaROOS. As the solo ended, Venus bent over and pulled something out of her

00:11:31.504 --> 00:11:32.420
shoe’s pouch.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:38.174
Three Texan brothers played guitars and sang in Members Only jackets, seven years

00:11:38.174 --> 00:11:43.568
past their prime, sweating through tit pockets and unbuttoned collars, a few

00:11:43.568 --> 00:11:49.250
minutes from giving their large, bronze belt buckles a salt ammonia patina. They

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:54.716
sang “Trying to be a hero, winding up a zero can scar a man right down to his

00:11:54.716 --> 00:12:00.542
soul, Living on the spotlight can kill a man outright, ‘cause all that glitters is

00:12:00.542 --> 00:12:06.224
not gold.” The eldest, Larry, had never read Aesop or Chaucer or The Merchant of

00:12:06.224 --> 00:12:11.114
Venice or heard Bob Marley’s Get up, Stand Up. He’d heard Stairway to

00:12:11.114 --> 00:12:16.724
Heaven—perhaps he just hadn’t copied it down quite right. He wore a white shirt

00:12:16.724 --> 00:12:22.262
with the American flag on it, and between songs started talking politics. “…Of

00:12:22.262 --> 00:12:28.160
course, me saying this will automatically brand me a racist. After all, a racist is

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:33.626
anyone is winning an argument with a liberal.” He would strum his electrified

00:12:33.626 --> 00:12:38.877
acoustic guitar, harmonize with his brothers, and wink at fans through the

00:12:38.877 --> 00:12:44.271
gradient brown tints of his glasses while tapping his boot. “When you put it

00:12:44.271 --> 00:12:49.953
on…something happens to you.” Donna was in the audience, thinking about what her

00:12:49.953 --> 00:12:55.563
grandmother would say. But she was also thinking about the hand on her hip, the

00:12:55.563 --> 00:13:00.957
color of the floor in dim light, the strobes in dry ice, about the last meal

00:13:00.957 --> 00:13:05.991
she ate, all the work she had to do on her research paper, the taste of

00:13:05.991 --> 00:13:11.458
Methedrine, and whether or not exit 168B on the I-5 would be closed after the

00:13:11.458 --> 00:13:12.033
concert.

00:13:12.033 --> 00:13:17.473
Waiting on the curb outside the Piazza del Duomo, E waited for a cab to take him

00:13:17.473 --> 00:13:22.706
to the airport. The sun was out, but it was raining, and the diagonally rowed

00:13:22.706 --> 00:13:27.732
brick street had puddles of standing water. People were carefully avoiding

00:13:27.732 --> 00:13:32.896
walking into the water, but the cars brought them splashes. E thought of N’s

00:13:32.896 --> 00:13:37.992
“When we see a waterfall, we think we see freedom of will and choice in the

00:13:37.992 --> 00:13:42.949
innumerable turnings, windings, breakings of the waves; but everything is

00:13:42.949 --> 00:13:47.425
necessary; each movement can be calculated mathematically.” He was

00:13:47.425 --> 00:13:52.383
surprised that he could recall that quote in English. Evidence of Milan’s

00:13:52.383 --> 00:13:57.891
restoration was everywhere, finished and in progress, and E noticed that the rain

00:13:57.891 --> 00:14:02.849
made the pink face of the Palazzo Carminati a little redder. By dusk, the

00:14:02.849 --> 00:14:07.118
east facade would be lit in neon—advertising not so much to the

00:14:07.118 --> 00:14:12.282
Milanese, but to the tourists that came to Northern Italy to shop for luxury

00:14:12.282 --> 00:14:17.377
goods within sight of the Alps. The storefronts that currently occupied the

00:14:17.377 --> 00:14:22.886
Carminati had redecorated significantly as the decade went on; while the city was

00:14:22.886 --> 00:14:27.706
primarily restoring things to their Renaissance glory. These shops were

00:14:27.706 --> 00:14:32.732
cutting their doors at odd angles, laminating counters with purple Formica

00:14:32.732 --> 00:14:37.965
with zigzag confetti, and hanging lime green signs with bold red lettering on

00:14:37.965 --> 00:14:42.992
them. E flagged a Radio Taxi, which abruptly changed direction to pick him

00:14:42.992 --> 00:14:48.363
up. The pastel blue and white 1950s FIAT Multipla seemed to bounce a bit on the

00:14:48.363 --> 00:14:53.389
bricks, displacing water in small, cascading waves as it approached. E had

00:14:53.389 --> 00:14:58.416
already gotten used to the fact that the Multipla looked like it was going

00:14:58.416 --> 00:15:03.649
backwards, that the driver would be at what looked like the back, a face that

00:15:03.649 --> 00:15:08.882
would hit air like a brick wall. The driver opened his door, which was hinged

00:15:08.882 --> 00:15:13.977
backward, and started packing E’s few small cases into the back through the

00:15:13.977 --> 00:15:19.348
large passenger doors. As he stepped into the cab and sat in its pink and green

00:15:19.348 --> 00:15:24.925
seats, piped in silver, E was glad to be done with Memphis, though he wasn’t quite

00:15:24.925 --> 00:15:30.434
sure it was done with him. He knew that the students in Seattle were going to ask

00:15:30.434 --> 00:15:35.391
him all kinds of stupid questions. Students always want him to talk about

00:15:35.391 --> 00:15:40.625
how he manages to keep everything fresh, to anticipate the new trend. At this

00:15:40.625 --> 00:15:45.926
moment he was pretty sure he was going to tell anyone who asked to “vaffanculo

00:15:45.926 --> 00:15:47.097
pezzo di merda.”.

00:15:47.097 --> 00:15:52.471
Chuck was stacking buckets on a shelf, considering the rhythm of white, grey,

00:15:52.471 --> 00:15:58.269
white, grey, small, two large, small, two large, grid. The only disruptions to this

00:15:58.269 --> 00:16:03.007
organization were the repackaged materials he’d scavenged into pink,

00:16:03.007 --> 00:16:08.381
yellow, and orange buckets, with less reflective finishes. One of the buckets

00:16:08.381 --> 00:16:13.472
fell, from the middle shelf. As the small, white cylinder rolled down the

00:16:13.472 --> 00:16:18.776
slightly graded concrete floor, he noticed the gel it contained spraying out

00:16:18.776 --> 00:16:24.432
of a now cracked lid, making a sideways, slow motion crown. The bucket passed the

00:16:24.432 --> 00:16:30.160
rolled up door and into bright sunlight, before coming to a stop at a small pebble

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:35.322
in his asphalt parking spot. As he peered over it, disappointed as only an

00:16:35.322 --> 00:16:40.837
overeducated and underachieving man can be, he saw the future: a blobby mass of

00:16:40.837 --> 00:16:46.565
cured, translucent plastic. He grabbed a screwdriver from his workbench, dipped it

00:16:46.565 --> 00:16:51.939
into the cracked bucket to gather a load of gel and watched it solidify as it

00:16:51.939 --> 00:16:57.667
dripped onto the asphalt—light arresting the viscous fluid as it fell. Back at the

00:16:57.667 --> 00:17:02.829
bench, he filled an upside down orange lid with this same photopolymer. He

00:17:02.829 --> 00:17:07.990
grabbed two plastic letters from an alphabet used to teach his daughter to

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:13.577
read some years prior, placed them on top of a sheet of glass from a small frame

00:17:13.577 --> 00:17:18.880
(that he hadn’t gotten around to placing a photo into,) and laid them on the

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.971
upturned lid. He sprayed the gel with light from a 4 cell Mag-Lite for 30

00:17:23.971 --> 00:17:29.416
seconds, removed the glass, and touched the contents. Everything that was soft

00:17:29.416 --> 00:17:34.790
moments before was hard, save the still goopy shadows of the letters V and P.

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:39.850
Sun blanched the once-magenta seats closest to the window, so much that the

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:45.252
piping on the back of the vinyl cushion had nearly disintegrated. Maggie settled

00:17:45.252 --> 00:17:50.380
into the padded cast-iron seat and swiveled to face her tray. Out the window

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:55.645
was a light blue Chevy Luv pickup truck with a sticker she could just make out

00:17:55.645 --> 00:18:01.252
as, “Like your Freedom? Thank a Vet.” She turned back to her book, “Individuals are

00:18:01.252 --> 00:18:06.244
elements of classes of individuals, classes of individuals are elements of

00:18:06.244 --> 00:18:11.646
classes of classes of individuals, and so on,” but then found herself staring at

00:18:11.646 --> 00:18:16.706
the cardstock box of fries which seemed to show a little too much white and

00:18:16.706 --> 00:18:22.108
yellow striped interior. “Fucking kids,” she muttered, as she leaned to her left

00:18:22.108 --> 00:18:27.373
side and disengaged two rubber straps at the top edges of a tall, narrow, red,

00:18:27.373 --> 00:18:32.706
plastic box, textured as semi-skin. She grabbed the red, soft-edged handle with

00:18:32.706 --> 00:18:37.698
her left hand and pulled a manual typewriter out of its integral case. The

00:18:37.698 --> 00:18:42.621
device was a second-hand gift from her aunt, Shelly Pazzi, who had nearly

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:48.160
entered the freshman class of 1970 at the University of Washington. S was the baby

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:53.493
sister of her family, with fifteen years between she and Maggie’s mother. S had

00:18:53.493 --> 00:18:58.143
been given the typewriter as a congratulatory gift when she was named

00:18:58.143 --> 00:19:03.340
valedictorian of the class of 1970 at Mount Si High School. But in the months

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:08.605
between her graduation and orientation, S had fallen in love with a boy from a

00:19:08.605 --> 00:19:13.460
neighboring town—and she put off her entrance to UW for a year. That was

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:18.725
sixteen collegeless years ago now. Maggie placed the pristine crimson Olivetti

00:19:18.725 --> 00:19:24.195
Valentine next to her tray in the empty restaurant, loaded a sheet of 20# Mohawk,

00:19:24.195 --> 00:19:29.255
and got to work on her first essay by smashing fry grease into the white on

00:19:29.255 --> 00:19:30.007
black keys.

00:19:30.007 --> 00:19:34.980
In 1986, you could still find motor oil in paperboard cans, although rounded

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:40.217
polyethylene bottles were becoming the norm. They were a little bit cheaper, and

00:19:40.217 --> 00:19:44.659
since people didn’t follow the manufacturer’s maintenance schedules,

00:19:44.659 --> 00:19:49.566
they didn’t tend to fly off store shelves. The Clark service station at the

00:19:49.566 --> 00:19:54.737
corner of Walton and Sashabaw was owned and managed by Joe P — who loved golden

00:19:54.737 --> 00:19:59.312
crosses, but wouldn’t have plastic bottles in his place. Joe had a big

00:19:59.312 --> 00:20:03.953
mustache, a gravelly voice, and considered himself to be a very ethical

00:20:03.953 --> 00:20:08.196
and principled person, despite occasionally sticking the emission

00:20:08.196 --> 00:20:13.036
testing probe in the tailpipe of a different car than the one his customer

00:20:13.036 --> 00:20:17.942
brought him to be inspected. While this could have been used as a lucrative

00:20:17.942 --> 00:20:23.047
technique to unnecessarily replace good catalytic converters or smog pumps and

00:20:23.047 --> 00:20:28.285
charcoal filters with new ones, Joe was more interested in getting his customers

00:20:28.285 --> 00:20:33.589
passing grades their cars hadn’t earned. And, as a result, his business did three

00:20:33.589 --> 00:20:38.893
times the number of oil changes, radiator flushes, and new tire sales than any of

00:20:38.893 --> 00:20:44.064
his competitors. Once a week he would dump the used motor oil in the field just

00:20:44.064 --> 00:20:49.103
east of the store, and burn the paper cans (still wet with brown Quaker State

00:20:49.103 --> 00:20:54.275
and Valvoline) in a large drum, all the while imagining burning the body of his

00:20:54.275 --> 00:20:54.805
ex-wife.

00:20:54.805 --> 00:21:00.390
While booze, the USSR, pot, MTV, integration, the IRS, crack, AIDS, and

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:06.613
crystal meth were scaring the shit out of everybody, most people died bad early

00:21:06.613 --> 00:21:13.076
deaths because of their wealth. They were working more, earning more, eating more,

00:21:13.076 --> 00:21:19.539
and stuffing it all into their arteries. Pepperoni pizza became the staple meal of

00:21:19.539 --> 00:21:25.762
the nuclear family, and they washed away thoughts of calories with Tab and Diet

00:21:25.762 --> 00:21:31.666
Coke. They did cycles of Deal-a-Meals, Jazzercises, and jogs with cycles of

00:21:31.666 --> 00:21:38.208
Absolut, McDonalds, and Jeopardy. Most of what they took in just moved through, but

00:21:38.208 --> 00:21:44.033
some of it stuck — and mountains of Raisin Bran before hours of Jet-Skiing

00:21:44.033 --> 00:21:49.139
couldn’t pass it. “Our history is an aggregate of last moments.”.

00:21:49.139 --> 00:21:54.468
“Every American is entitled to Life, Liberty, and the Pursuit of Trivia,” the

00:21:54.468 --> 00:21:59.937
advertisement read, near the bottom of the page. A man dressed in a baggy white

00:21:59.937 --> 00:22:05.055
coat looked over the full page, thinking about the six new card sets being

00:22:05.055 --> 00:22:10.314
described, and how many more sets he’d have to develop before the end of the

00:22:10.314 --> 00:22:15.853
decade. He flipped through the rest of that issue of Time, closed it, and placed

00:22:15.853 --> 00:22:21.322
it on a relatively empty shelf, crowded with photographs of his children. Three

00:22:21.322 --> 00:22:25.879
different complete encyclopedias interspersed various recordbooks,

00:22:25.879 --> 00:22:31.138
factbooks, novels, biographies, and periodicals on the other shelves. A copy

00:22:31.138 --> 00:22:36.467
of the April 14 issue of the New York Times was splayed on his desk, with the

00:22:36.467 --> 00:22:41.445
words “any alleged theological justification for discriminatory measures

00:22:41.445 --> 00:22:47.054
or, worse still, for acts of persecution is unfounded” underlined. He had come on

00:22:47.054 --> 00:22:52.383
board at the firm as they were about to launch what would become the defining

00:22:52.383 --> 00:22:57.782
game in a decade that was defined by games. Video games may have been the most

00:22:57.782 --> 00:23:02.620
disruptive category for children, but Trivial Pursuit was the crowning

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:08.229
pseudosmart pastime for the adult middle classes. “How do we handle controversial

00:23:08.229 --> 00:23:13.418
subjects?” he asked himself, while flipping through the Selchow and Righter

00:23:13.418 --> 00:23:18.536
company telephone directory. When he pressed #934, he heard a busy signal.

00:23:18.536 --> 00:23:23.908
Shelly could count back change, but she couldn’t tell the difference between Dire

00:23:23.908 --> 00:23:28.675
Straits and Men and Work. She was patient, funny, and competent—she knew

00:23:28.675 --> 00:23:33.375
the extensions for every department at K-Mart by heart. She would feign

00:23:33.375 --> 00:23:38.210
excitement during every blue light special, and happily organized Michael

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:43.380
Jackson, Tina Turner, and Bon Jovi clad school folders on the endcaps when she

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:48.618
didn’t have a line of customers. But she HATED the new registers. The NCR Tower

00:23:48.618 --> 00:23:53.721
architecture was difficult enough to adjust to, having stripped away from the

00:23:53.721 --> 00:23:58.958
representative the skill of reading a price tag, in favor of scanning a printed

00:23:58.958 --> 00:24:03.659
barcode. But the NCR-3360 was infuriating. She sat in on a presentation

00:24:03.659 --> 00:24:08.560
before they were installed and was given a round white button with a black

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:13.731
silhouette and the word “panther” on it by the technical representative. “Am I

00:24:13.731 --> 00:24:19.035
supposed to love this thing so much that I would wear it as jewelry?” she asked,

00:24:19.035 --> 00:24:24.273
but was not answered. She thought about what her mother would say to her if she

00:24:24.273 --> 00:24:28.772
complained about the new systems at Thanksgiving dinner. Her younger

00:24:28.772 --> 00:24:34.009
coworkers had no problem adjusting to the new systems, but Shelly just couldn’t

00:24:34.009 --> 00:24:38.844
find what she was looking for, “this thing has a million buttons.” To her

00:24:38.844 --> 00:24:43.410
knowledge, the 3360s never made arithmetical errors, but she couldn’t

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:48.513
bring herself to take direction from a CRT’s script—she insisted on returning

00:24:48.513 --> 00:24:53.482
remainders in the same way she’d been taught in her home economics class at

00:24:53.482 --> 00:24:55.966
CHS: “And twenty-five makes twenty.”.

00:24:55.966 --> 00:25:01.334
The Alaskan earth shook like a snapped towel, leaving everything in the path of

00:25:01.334 --> 00:25:06.495
this particular shudder momentarily weightless as the sound-breaking seismic

00:25:06.495 --> 00:25:11.106
wave unmoored things from their foundations. Soil on Atka Island was

00:25:11.106 --> 00:25:16.060
liquified in the summer, and tiny tsunamis meowed around the Pacific. The

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:21.153
mini storage facilities at the research lab were a specialized form of pole

00:25:21.153 --> 00:25:26.727
building: thick, treated columns buried, and either cemented in or set on concrete

00:25:26.727 --> 00:25:31.269
pads placed into the bottoms of eight-foot-deep postholes. A simple

00:25:31.269 --> 00:25:36.568
lintel header system, called a truss carrier, connected each of these vertical

00:25:36.568 --> 00:25:41.936
beams. Horizontal runners spanned the gaps below the carriers, placed every few

00:25:41.936 --> 00:25:47.510
feet, to which the painted steel cladding was attached. As the building fell away,

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:52.809
thirteen crates in unit 14A were exposed for the first time in a year. Stacked

00:25:52.809 --> 00:25:57.832
three across, five deep, and two high—some of the barrels in the upper row

00:25:57.832 --> 00:26:03.269
started to tumble as the debris landed on them, and one of the lids burst off. A

00:26:03.269 --> 00:26:08.912
man in a wheelchair, with legs long since amputated from a bout with bilateral bone

00:26:08.912 --> 00:26:13.660
cancer, dusted off his 82nd Airborne hat, lit a cigar, and dropped it.

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:18.875
Pearl had been dying her hair since she was in her late twenties. She was now

00:26:18.875 --> 00:26:24.432
past fifty, with a late mid-life bouffant the color of jet. She hated every second

00:26:24.432 --> 00:26:29.235
of dealing with teenagers. Her semi-office contained a silver and green

00:26:29.235 --> 00:26:34.381
Praxis 48 typewriter, an AB Dick 217 spirit duplicator with a broken platen,

00:26:34.381 --> 00:26:39.527
an orange Steelcase Tanker desk, a bookshelf that sagged under the weight of

00:26:39.527 --> 00:26:44.673
twenty-two 3” three ring binders (with typed label cards in their spines,) a

00:26:44.673 --> 00:26:49.682
commemorative glass Christmas cup from Arby’s filled with yellow hexagonal

00:26:49.682 --> 00:26:54.485
pencils, a framed photograph of her daughter held up on her desk with a

00:26:54.485 --> 00:26:59.837
kickstand, and a custom made plaque on the wall that displayed a duplicate copy

00:26:59.837 --> 00:27:04.983
of a Masters of Ed diploma from Wayne State University. It was signed by the

00:27:04.983 --> 00:27:10.541
Provost with facsimile signatures of the president of the university, as well as a

00:27:10.541 --> 00:27:15.961
few deans and the chair of the elected board of governors. This diploma referred

00:27:15.961 --> 00:27:20.833
to Pearl’s boss, Ron Kuski. Her mother commented on it the few times she

00:27:20.833 --> 00:27:26.390
visited, she was disappointed that Pearl hadn’t finished her first semester at OU.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:31.399
She opened the bottom of her three drawers, pulled out a pistol with three

00:27:31.399 --> 00:27:36.751
arrows on its grip from a cleaning fluid soaked rag, and calmly stuffed it into

00:27:36.751 --> 00:27:37.437
her purse.

00:27:37.437 --> 00:27:42.544
Jim Pazzi waited in a hallway for word on his wife’s condition from the nurses,

00:27:42.544 --> 00:27:47.912
most of whom were at the ends of fourteen hours shifts. Norfolk county had recently

00:27:47.912 --> 00:27:52.626
passed an ordinance that had the effect of banning smoking in all medical

00:27:52.626 --> 00:27:57.732
facilities, but his brothers and their wives smoked him out of the waiting room

00:27:57.732 --> 00:28:02.643
anyway. At the east end of the hall, away from the patient rooms and nurse’s

00:28:02.643 --> 00:28:07.815
station, he studied a framed image on a tan papered wall. A small march of women

00:28:07.815 --> 00:28:12.856
held mostly illegible banners, wore hats, and had on what appeared to be white

00:28:12.856 --> 00:28:18.093
sashes atop their black coats. The only words Jim could make out in the old black

00:28:18.093 --> 00:28:22.480
and white photograph were “NO 5, B ISRAEL, PITAL, O T 1911,” each on

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:27.455
separate lines. Beneath the image a small brass label, nearly covered in oily

00:28:27.455 --> 00:28:31.907
fingerprints, read “The Beth Israel Hospital Association received the

00:28:31.907 --> 00:28:36.752
greatest part of its initial support from the Greater Boston Jewish Women’s

00:28:36.752 --> 00:28:41.924
Auxillary, who pioneered the struggle to establish a hospital to serve the needs

00:28:41.924 --> 00:28:47.227
of the growing immigrant population.” His grandmother had been a part of a similar

00:28:47.227 --> 00:28:52.399
organization when she was growing up in Detroit, but they were committed only to

00:28:52.399 --> 00:28:57.637
the Catholic immigrants. Jim saw a nurse coming toward him and he decided that he

00:28:57.637 --> 00:29:02.678
would do whatever the doctor recommended, including aborting their fetus if it

00:29:02.678 --> 00:29:04.511
meant saving his wife, Abby.

00:29:04.511 --> 00:29:10.060
MG waited at a brown table, surrounded by glass, bombarded by radiation from the

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:15.749
Burbank sun. He was wearing a cornflower blue shirt with a light checkered pattern

00:29:15.749 --> 00:29:21.368
beneath a slightly reflective black sport coat. His glasses were folded next to a

00:29:21.368 --> 00:29:26.636
small notebook. The VP of corporate affairs was now twenty minutes late, and

00:29:26.636 --> 00:29:32.255
MG had to tell the receptionist for the third time that (although he loved coffee

00:29:32.255 --> 00:29:37.523
pots) he did not drink coffee, and was fine waiting without refreshments. He

00:29:37.523 --> 00:29:42.581
smiled the whole time, his right hand incredibly steady, holding a simple

00:29:42.581 --> 00:29:47.919
disposable pen, making sketchy marks. His lines were nonsense until they were

00:29:47.919 --> 00:29:53.327
brilliant. What started out as a series of stuttering, wavy lines were layered

00:29:53.327 --> 00:29:58.314
together until clear, subdivided rectangles with six pomoerotic dwarves

00:29:58.314 --> 00:30:03.512
appeared, hands above their heads. They held up the hypotenuse of an obtuse

00:30:03.512 --> 00:30:08.709
triangle with a smaller dwarf inside, holding up the vertex. That, in turn,

00:30:08.709 --> 00:30:13.626
supported a rectangle, and an ever so slightly tilted dome in one point

00:30:13.626 --> 00:30:19.105
perspective, with the vanishing point on the horizon line. The executive walked

00:30:19.105 --> 00:30:24.373
into the room and MG rose to greet him, confident that he could use the VP’s

00:30:24.373 --> 00:30:26.199
lateness to his advantage.

00:30:26.199 --> 00:30:31.761
“When we complain, we remain. When we whine, we stay behind. When we praise, we

00:30:31.761 --> 00:30:37.608
raise,” a middle aged man in the TV said, moments before he introduced his daughter

00:30:37.608 --> 00:30:43.312
to the studio audience. Tammy Sue’s hair was darker and BIGGER than Tammy Faye’s.

00:30:43.312 --> 00:30:48.517
It covered her entire forehead, her shoulders, and most of the set she was

00:30:48.517 --> 00:30:54.151
standing on. “If you’re looking for that special something, and your heart is on

00:30:54.151 --> 00:30:59.855
the ground, only one Grace can be found,” she lip synced, emphatically, but still

00:30:59.855 --> 00:31:05.132
unconvincingly. The microphone was far too small for her to completely hide

00:31:05.132 --> 00:31:10.693
behind. Unseen background singers tried to fill the empty space, Tammy Sue kind

00:31:10.693 --> 00:31:15.756
of clapped, and drum machines kind of kept the beat. As the horrid music

00:31:15.756 --> 00:31:20.961
finished, and the camera returned to Tammy Sue’s father, still seated on a

00:31:20.961 --> 00:31:26.167
pink, white, and blue floral couch, he started talking about the calls and

00:31:26.167 --> 00:31:31.800
letters of support he’d been getting in the past few months. Jim Bakker recently

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:37.433
lost weight and had a full head of sweaty hair. The top two buttons of his shirt

00:31:37.433 --> 00:31:43.208
were unbuttoned under his textured white jacked with large shoulder pads. “I think

00:31:43.208 --> 00:31:48.485
the enemy planned this at the time that we would be down, when we were just

00:31:48.485 --> 00:31:53.904
getting back up again, getting ready to minister. We have no one else to lean

00:31:53.904 --> 00:31:59.395
on.” The two Tammys leaned in to hold him, and a woman in Royal Oak, Michigan,

00:31:59.395 --> 00:32:01.819
on her white linen couch, giggled.

00:32:01.819 --> 00:32:07.174
A man sat in his office, staring out of his window onto an old factory floor, 15

00:32:07.174 --> 00:32:12.597
feet below. The shop was divided into a number of different zones; closest to his

00:32:12.597 --> 00:32:17.613
office was a large space that the Marketing department was using to prepare

00:32:17.613 --> 00:32:22.900
for upcoming public demonstrations, the area beyond was a bay dedicated to Beta

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:27.780
III vehicles that were being prepped for EPA and DOT inspections, and the

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:32.796
remaining eighty percent of the plant contained rows of numbered parts bins

00:32:32.796 --> 00:32:37.541
waiting to be moved to the assembly lines. In the Marketing area, three

00:32:37.541 --> 00:32:42.625
identical triplets in shapely white jumpsuits were in the middle of removing

00:32:42.625 --> 00:32:47.979
plastic panels from a new mid-engined, 2-seat commuter car that was undergoing a

00:32:47.979 --> 00:32:52.114
set of mid-model aesthetic changes. They stacked the flame red

00:32:52.114 --> 00:32:57.334
reaction-injection-molded polyurethane panels on a rack and replaced them with

00:32:57.334 --> 00:33:02.553
Talbot yellow panels that included a fastback roofline. The man wasn’t staring

00:33:02.553 --> 00:33:07.704
at the triplets, or thinking about how much they looked like his daughter, or

00:33:07.704 --> 00:33:12.449
their team of makeup artists, choreographers, and wardrobe specialists.

00:33:12.449 --> 00:33:17.533
He was looking through all of them, past the stage, into the concrete floor,

00:33:17.533 --> 00:33:22.684
organizing part numbers, vendor contact names, and engineering revisions into

00:33:22.684 --> 00:33:28.175
labeled storage bins in his cortex. Some contained facial expressions and receipts

00:33:28.175 --> 00:33:33.598
remembered from Christmas gifts given to his step-children in prior years, others

00:33:33.598 --> 00:33:38.817
held the names of actresses that he had entered into a notebook when he was in

00:33:38.817 --> 00:33:43.765
his teens. At this moment he was thinking about the bits of the ROM in the

00:33:43.765 --> 00:33:48.578
tube-launched, optically tracked, and wire-guided antitank missiles he’d

00:33:48.578 --> 00:33:54.068
developed a few years before he’d joined the Pontiac engineering department. “What

00:33:54.068 --> 00:33:58.745
is the consequence of having all this information stored in such close

00:33:58.745 --> 00:34:03.897
proximity?” A television was talking behind him, showing the mugshot of a man

00:34:03.897 --> 00:34:05.998
in a suit with the DOB 10 7 43.

00:34:05.998 --> 00:34:07.484
To Whom it May Concern,

00:34:07.484 --> 00:34:12.487
I am writing in response to a May 13 editorial that appeared in Catholic

00:34:12.487 --> 00:34:18.053
Answers, with the title “Not Pretty in Punk.” I found Father Nagel’s argument to

00:34:18.053 --> 00:34:23.619
be a medicine for our ailing nation, and have been hand copying this article out

00:34:23.619 --> 00:34:28.481
as a kind of verbal vaccine, and sending it to each of my children and

00:34:28.481 --> 00:34:33.554
grandchildren—with the request that they discuss this important topic, to

00:34:33.554 --> 00:34:39.049
inoculate them against these evils. I am a proud 1938 graduate of the School of

00:34:39.049 --> 00:34:44.263
the Shrine of the Little Flower, and I found this erudite warning about the

00:34:44.263 --> 00:34:49.759
infectious nature of popular films on our children to be very much in line with

00:34:49.759 --> 00:34:55.325
what Father Coughlin taught us. As your readers doubtless know, by the 1930s the

00:34:55.325 --> 00:35:00.539
Father had come to see his earlier support for liberal policies as the sins

00:35:00.539 --> 00:35:05.965
that they were—and he taught us to avoid the false promises of socialist ideas

00:35:05.965 --> 00:35:11.672
that were not in the best interest of the moral class, and which were too friendly

00:35:11.672 --> 00:35:17.238
to Israelite bankers. Father Nagel echoes these same concerns, “These unreading,

00:35:17.238 --> 00:35:22.522
unshaven, unrepentent teenagers with their fishnet fabrics and their violent

00:35:22.522 --> 00:35:27.595
haircuts are victims. They are not victims of neglect, nor their own free

00:35:27.595 --> 00:35:33.021
choices, but of coercive pushers of a bankrupt culture desperately looking for

00:35:33.021 --> 00:35:38.446
fresh currency.” He is right to compare films of this kind, and the people who

00:35:38.446 --> 00:35:43.660
watch and follow them, as living in a ‘highly textured wilderness mirage.’.

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:48.593
Some people say these are just benign choices of clothing, and music, and

00:35:48.593 --> 00:35:53.526
films, but culture is the basis of ethical behavior. What they learn from

00:35:53.526 --> 00:35:58.870
Cindy Lauper, Molly Ringwald, and Vivian Westwood is to scream and cry and pose

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:04.145
when they should be studying, listening, and practicing those skills that will

00:36:04.145 --> 00:36:09.078
make them useful as mothers and knowledgeable citizens and lay ministers.

00:36:09.078 --> 00:36:14.354
Instead, they are given the Centurion’s spear to stab our Savior. Let us lobby

00:36:14.354 --> 00:36:19.287
our schools to again provide moral education for all of our students, not

00:36:19.287 --> 00:36:24.631
just those who happen to have been lucky enough to be born Catholic, or to find

00:36:24.631 --> 00:36:29.906
the one true Church in their childhoods. Father Coughlin gave the commencement

00:36:29.906 --> 00:36:35.387
address to my granddaughter’s graduating class in 1978, and I will never forget a

00:36:35.387 --> 00:36:40.731
word of his remarks—among them, “It is the State which educates its citizens in

00:36:40.731 --> 00:36:45.390
civic virtue, gives them the consciousness of their mission and welds

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:50.734
them into unity.” I am so happy to read that there are others left in our great

00:36:50.734 --> 00:36:55.804
country who recognize the diseases that infect our youth, and are preparing

00:36:55.804 --> 00:36:58.613
ointments to save their bodies and souls.

00:36:58.613 --> 00:36:59.681
Fiat lux,

00:36:59.681 --> 00:37:02.142
Donatella Pazzi.

00:37:02.142 --> 00:37:07.897
The face we see in song is a phantasm of invention, sucking Muse’s marrow, less

00:37:07.897 --> 00:37:13.579
than death. The soft rectangle somehow humanizes the glowing tube — but as the

00:37:13.579 --> 00:37:19.112
signal indication widens, the volume button collapses to a point. The source

00:37:19.112 --> 00:37:24.351
of the sound is powerful so long as it exists in silence — but its power

00:37:24.351 --> 00:37:29.959
diminishes as the minstrel fills the medium. Things are gods until we can see

00:37:29.959 --> 00:37:35.862
the fingerprints of their makers, until we can feel them trying to manipulate us,

00:37:35.862 --> 00:37:41.912
until they carry water for other gods. As our attention flips from craft to content

00:37:41.912 --> 00:37:47.003
we hear cries of agency drowning agents, pretty noise snaps into sign.

00:37:47.003 --> 00:37:52.498
We are emperors, surrounded by subjects who share our delusions. They see our

00:37:52.498 --> 00:37:58.282
sheer accoutrements, but naming them will open curtains on their own nakedness. A

00:37:58.282 --> 00:38:03.633
seventy-something-year-old man sits, staring out of a window while eating a

00:38:03.633 --> 00:38:09.200
sandwich. He is wearing a desert camo hat decorated with a stamped bronze USMC

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:14.695
medallion. His black graphic T says, “There are wolves. There are sheep. I am

00:38:14.695 --> 00:38:20.262
the sheep dog.” This is his mascara: a militant costume for an impotent former

00:38:20.262 --> 00:38:26.191
soldier. My makeup is footwear: a bright, shiny thing to draw attention from my fat

00:38:26.191 --> 00:38:31.758
and lazy ignorance. There is no boundary between enhancement and disguise—they

00:38:31.758 --> 00:38:37.036
have the same effects. Our makeup is transformative, opaque, to be seen by

00:38:37.036 --> 00:38:42.820
anyone (who isn’t hopelessly stupid) as style, beauty, and strength. The shepherd

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:48.315
eating a sheep sandwich thinks he is a hero in slip-on cow loafers cum combat

00:38:48.315 --> 00:38:51.786
boots, and I think my CK quarter zip is slimming.

00:38:51.786 --> 00:38:57.715
We’re slowing down for fear of winter and are storing only breath. We hold nervous,

00:38:57.715 --> 00:39:03.355
wavering postures; erect because we dare not exhale. September is less one day,

00:39:03.355 --> 00:39:08.778
and we’ve fallen before leaves; from steady to still. We are primed to shake

00:39:08.778 --> 00:39:13.912
off this paralysis, but lack will. We’ve calm lungs, but not for lack of

00:39:13.912 --> 00:39:14.635
screaming.

00:39:14.635 --> 00:39:20.384
Reactors, dams, conversations, and unshoed feet fill homes with electricity.

00:39:20.384 --> 00:39:25.903
We move from person to person, room to room. Mouths scream politics, kiss

00:39:25.903 --> 00:39:32.036
religion, quiver at gossip, and whisper affections. Temperatures change, air gets

00:39:32.036 --> 00:39:38.015
more or less saturated with water, and carpet turns to rug to hard wood. It’s a

00:39:38.015 --> 00:39:43.687
system demarcated from the next by an icon, the modern object fulfilling an

00:39:43.687 --> 00:39:49.513
ancient commandment. A tiny silver vessel containing a scroll most will never

00:39:49.513 --> 00:39:55.338
touch, marked with words few more can read. It affects every passing pulse of

00:39:55.338 --> 00:39:57.638
light; taking oil from prints.

00:39:57.638 --> 00:40:02.852
One part of the chemistry of a household. Grounded by the screws that keep it from

00:40:02.852 --> 00:40:07.744
the floor, charged at the very point where it is touched. A cast, fabricated,

00:40:07.744 --> 00:40:11.477
and electrified object that’s part of a reactive structure.

00:40:11.477 --> 00:40:17.526
Cold doesn’t come, heat goes. Armageddon isn’t a battlefield of soldiers; it’s a

00:40:17.526 --> 00:40:23.346
dark, low energy tundra. It is a space where atoms toddle in objects at rest,

00:40:23.346 --> 00:40:29.242
finally able to relax into shards and blobs. The end is only an end because we

00:40:29.242 --> 00:40:35.368
have a word for it, an end for us. Heat is the waste of flowers being pollinated,

00:40:35.368 --> 00:40:41.494
love for cherubs, and the light we write our bibles by. It’s the infrared heat in

00:40:41.494 --> 00:40:47.160
the panel that sets the ink on a Stryper T-shirt, and the fire that keeps a

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:53.133
grandmother from death. It will never be extinguished, but it will average out.

00:40:53.133 --> 00:40:58.549
Progress wasn’t made by individuals, groups, races, or nations, but by matter,

00:40:58.549 --> 00:41:04.316
enslaved by man. The hero and villain are innovation and artifact — in the hands of

00:41:04.316 --> 00:41:09.732
humans, but outlasting us. It’s the ink, cloth, dye, blade, alloy, fire, mine,

00:41:09.732 --> 00:41:14.725
glaze, and structure who willed their objects into our vaults — and they

00:41:14.725 --> 00:41:19.578
survive to write the world. Every engineer dies; their expired patents

00:41:19.578 --> 00:41:24.994
tilting as epitaphs. Designers are dozed into earth before their patterns. The

00:41:24.994 --> 00:41:30.269
one-of-a-kind has a half life that is exponentially longer than its artists,

00:41:30.269 --> 00:41:35.755
buried dozens a dime. “All men would be tyrants if they could,” so we are kept,

00:41:35.755 --> 00:41:41.171
elementally, from extending our despotism into eternities by mass in quivering

00:41:41.171 --> 00:41:41.663
revolt.
